    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. so the ldl is basically just hanging around in the blood and sticking to the walls? does it like, slowly eat away at the arteries or something? i'm just trying to visualize how it actually causes damage.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, LDL particles infiltrate and stick to the artery walls, triggering an inflammatory response that creates plaque. Over time, this plaque builds up and narrows the arteries, but it doesn't actively \"eat away\" the tissue in a prehistoric sense. Instead, the buildup restricts blood flow and can eventually rupture to cause a dangerous heart attack or stroke. Think of it more like a slow clogging rather than a destructive eating process.",
