    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay i get the clogging part. but if it's not \"eating\" the tissue, what exactly is happening during that inflammatory part? like, is it just a pile of junk sitting there or is there something more active going on that causes the damage? it feels like something is causing trouble in there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When LDL clings to the artery wall, it tricks white blood cells into lining up behind it, but because LDL is toxic, these immune cells infiltrate the area and become chaotic \"foam cells\" filled with junk. This triggers a release of chemicals that creates inflamed plaques, which can cause crises like a heart attack if the protective outer layer ruptures during stress. It is an active, inflammatory build-up rather than passive clogging that poses the real danger. Supporting your doctor's advice with diet and exercise is a great first step to managing this process.",
      "source": "target",
